  "metadata": {
    "provenance": "Computed from HCES 2023-24 unit-level microdata. The value of items received free through social-welfare programmes is reconstructed using MOSPI's own imputation-rate book (HCES_2023_24_Imputation_Rate.xlsx, DDI catalog 237): free-food quantities (LEVEL-05 item codes 061-075, value blank in the raw data) and free-durable counts (LEVEL-11 Num_Free_* columns, dummy codes 991-997) are multiplied by the state x sector rate (basis: mode).",
    "method": "Without-imputation MPCE is the standard LEVEL-14 reconstruction, calibrated to the published national rural/urban averages (Rs 4,122 / Rs 6,996). The imputation gap is the per-capita imputed value of free welfare items, on the same calibration scale. Fractiles are population-weighted (household multiplier x household size), ranked on without-imputation MPCE, computed separately for rural and urban.",
    "calibration": {
      "rural": {
        "withoutImputation": 4122,
        "averageGap": 121.1,
        "publishedWithout": 4122,
        "publishedGap": 125
      },
      "urban": {
        "withoutImputation": 6996,
        "averageGap": 82.2,
        "publishedWithout": 6996,
        "publishedGap": 82
      },
      "gatePass": true,
      "rateBasis": "mode"
    },
    "caveat": "Welfare-free items are reconstructed, not read off a single column: free-food rows carry quantity but no value, so value is imputed at MOSPI's published state x sector rate. Durable counts are a 365-day stock monthlyised by /12. Fractile boundaries and levels depend on the MPCE reconstruction and reference-period scaling; treat the gap-by-fractile pattern as indicative."
  }
}
